.TH CTPOOL_DESTROY 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ctpool_destroy \- destroy a thread pool and invalidate the handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

ctpool_destroy(pool);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Drains or cancels every task that is still pending, joins every worker
thread, waits until every resolved use of the handle that is still in
flight has stopped, and frees every resource of the pool. Then it sets
the pool handle to CTPOOL_NULL.

If the caller called neither ctpool_shutdown_drain() nor
ctpool_shutdown_immediate() before, the macro runs a drain shutdown
itself, so every task in the queue finishes before the pool is freed.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
pool  Thread pool variable to destroy (set to CTPOOL_NULL after
      destruction).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Calling it when pool is CTPOOL_NULL is safe, and the macro then does
nothing.

A call on a stale handle (one that an earlier call already destroyed,
or that another thread destroys at the same time) is a FATAL ERROR,
which means abort() and SIGABRT, and never a silent double free. The
handle carries a generation tag that the library checks before it
touches the pool, so such a value is always detected and never
dereferenced.

A call on pool from inside a task running on one of pool's worker
threads, or from the on_complete callback of that task, is a FATAL
ERROR as well, for a related reason: that worker is still on its way
back through its own dispatch loop, and it would touch the pool's
mutex, condition variables and struct right after this call freed
them. Leave the destroy to another thread, or run it after the task
returns.

The macro evaluates pool exactly once. It must be a modifiable lvalue,
such as a variable or an element of an array, so with an expression
that has a side effect, such as handles[i++], it destroys and clears
one element.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_construct (3),
.BR ctpool_shutdown_drain (3),
.BR ctpool_shutdown_immediate (3)
